Date: Tue, 9 Mar 1993 14:21:48 -0800 (PST)
From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: ECON/NANO/PHIL: Drive to reproduce

Tom Morrow:
> I believe that many humans have an inborn desire to nurture
> infants.

This is not the same thing as a desire to _have_ an infant -- not
even close.  The infant/mother bonding thing may be strong enough
to elicit envy in some cases where a woman (and perhaps a man too, though
it doesn't happen to any men I know) is often exposed to a neighbor's
or freind's baby.  And indeed, fertility often seems to be a fad, as
in the "baby boom" (a temporary and partial regression to 
pre-birth-control fertility levels).  But that's just a meme, and
it has to compete with all sorts of other memes just as or more
motivational, such as the "I want a fast car" meme, "I want a 
big roomy house" meme, "I wouldn't be sexy with my belly swelled
up" meme, "the world is overpopulated" meme, "my career would 
suffer" meme, etc.  Before reliable birth control, there was no
effective way for these other memes to prevent fertility.

But you don't have to believe me; just study the demographics.
Look at cultures with effective birth control vs. those without.
Look at families who could easily feed ten children, (ie: they
have over ten times the income of poor families) and see how
many children they actually have. Try to come up with as many twisted 
explanations as you can about how these folks are being Darwinianly 
Correct and "maximizing their genetic fitness" in some ambiguous or
mystical way; the fact of the matter is that they are not even replacing
themselves, and are up to an order of magnitude short of their
potential reproduction rate, were the genes really in control.

> Interesting:  I read once that only two words among
> many played at high speed consistently  caught the subtly-measured
> attention of human listeners:  sex and baby.

These are the two of the most common words in rock music, but the "baby"
does not refer to infants, not even close.  Musicians crooning
about how much they want infants should be quite common according to 
your theory, but it's been a long time since I've heard one. 

Nick Szabo				szabo@techbook.com

